{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# S5 — Bound writable container layers and alarm on exited-container debris\n\n## Why\nWritable container layers are each container's overlay2 `upperdir` inside `/var/lib/docker`.\nThey grow independently of image layers and BuildKit cache, no prune reaches them, and nothing\nmeasured them on 2026-09-02 — the only signal was \"root disk high\". Last cap under ADR 0112.\n\n## Mechanism decision, with evidence\nThe ticket offers a runtime storage quota \"when compatible\", else a reaper. **The quota is NOT\ncompatible here.** overlay2's `--storage-opt size=` (and the `overlay2.size` daemon default) is\naccepted only when the filesystem backing `/var/lib/docker` is XFS mounted with `pquota`;\notherwise the daemon refuses: `--storage-opt is supported only for overlay over xfs with\n'pquota' mount option`. That filesystem is ROOT here, and XFS reads quota options at MOUNT time\nand refuses them on a remount — so it needs `rootflags=pquota` and a REBOOT. Same constraint S4\n(`2ba3`) hit for `/var/tmp`, re-derived and binding here. Two further limits even after that\nreboot: the quota is PER-CONTAINER, not an aggregate ceiling, and applies at creation, so every\nlive service must be recreated. So this ships the mitigation, publishes which regime the box is\nin, and puts the enablement steps in the runbook.\n\n## What the reaper does NOT guarantee\n1. **It cannot reclaim a RUNNING container's writable layer at all.** Only exited/dead\n   containers are removable, so for the live compose set this is MEASUREMENT AND AN ALARM, not a\n   bound. Only the reboot-gated quota would bound those. Sharper than S4's fill rate, said first.\n2. For the debris it does bound, the bound is `cap + fill_rate x interval`. At the 2 GiB share\n   and a 300 s period a sustained net fill above ~7.2 MB/s outruns it; this gp3 volume does\n   125 MB/s baseline, ~17x that.\n3. Nothing that exited inside the grace window (900 s) is removed, so a burst of fresh exits is\n   unreclaimable by design. When it cannot get under the share it SAYS SO.\n\n## Safety — proved against the live set\n`docker container prune --filter until=…` cannot express what this host needs: its filters are\n`until` and `label`, with NO name filter. `autodeploy.sh` starts the mcp blue-green backends\nwith a bare `docker run` compose never sees, and reaps them itself in `mcp_retire_sweep` under a\nguard this reaper cannot replicate — it reads the nginx `/mcp/` upstream include and refuses to\nreap an exited container that is still the live backend, because `--restart always` would have\nrestored it. Reaping one anyway is bug `9ea3`: a transient exit became a permanent 502 on\n2026-09-02. So candidates are enumerated and removed individually, sparing:\n\n* anything labelled `com.docker.compose.project` (gerrit, review-bot, opcert, compose-mcp-1) —\n  an exited compose service is a crashed service whose logs are the evidence;\n* anything labelled `rebar.service` (`mcp_run_new`'s stable identity);\n* anything matching `^(rebar-mcp|compose-mcp-1)` — `mcp_managed`'s own regex, so the two agree.\n\nTwo daemon-enforced guarantees, not shell logic: candidates come from `docker ps -a --filter\nstatus=exited --filter status=dead`, which never lists a running container; removal is\n`docker rm` WITHOUT `-f`, which the daemon refuses for a running container (the precedent\n`mcp_retire_image` already relies on). No `-v`/`--volumes`; `docker system prune` never used.\n\n## What invokes the reaper, and what publishes the heartbeat\nTwo DIFFERENT callers; naming only one is how a heartbeat ends up with no producer.\n* The REAPER runs from its own `rebar-container-reaper.timer` (5 min period, `TimeoutStartSec`\n  nested strictly below it per bug `1205`), rendered by `--print-units` and installed by\n  `compose-up.sh` beside the three caps it already installs. A timer, not a hook in the\n  autodeploy tick, because `prune_docker_caches` runs only on deploy/backoff/pressure paths, so\n  a quiescent `main` would never reap.\n* The HEARTBEAT is published by `observability.sh`, whose 5-minute timer already exists\n  (`install-observability.sh`); §2i calls `--check-active`, which is side-effect-free. So \"every\n  tick\" is the OBSERVABILITY tick, and the heartbeat has a producer even when the reaper timer\n  is the thing that is dead — which is the point of it.\n\n## Observability (S2's publish-or-stay-silent rule)\n§2i, from the SAME single `docker system df` call §2f already makes — the format string gains\n`{{.Reclaimable}}` rather than adding a second daemon walk:\n`container_writable_bytes` (Containers Size), `container_exited_bytes` (Containers\nReclaimable), `container_writable_used_percent` (`pct_of_cap`, S3's helper, reused),\n`container_reaper_active` (1/0 heartbeat), `container_quota_enforceable` (1/0: is the kernel\nenforcing XFS project quota on the Docker root fs, i.e. could a ceiling exist at all).\nNon-heartbeat readings publish ONLY on a successful measurement.\n\n**Field surface verified — MATCH.** `docker system df --format` exposes `{{.Reclaimable}}`; on\nthe Containers row `Size` is the sum of writable layers (`SizeRw`) and `Reclaimable` is the\nsubset belonging to non-running containers, i.e. exactly the stopped-container debris. Checked\nagainst the CLI reference and Docker's disk-usage docs before changing the format string. An\nunrecognised row or missing third field publishes NOTHING for the affected reading, so a future\nrendering change degrades to silence-that-pages, never to a wrong number.\n\n## Alarms (`monitoring_autodeploy.tf`)\n`rebar-container-writable-usage-high` (>85%) and `rebar-container-reaper-not-active` (<1).\n**The second pre-empts the S4 finding**: without it a silently-dead timer leaves usage reading\nnominal while nothing enforces anything. Both `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`, both wired to\n`aws_sns_topic.alerts`. `container_quota_enforceable` is published WITHOUT an alarm: its honest\nvalue is 0 until the reboot, and an alarm that pages continuously is muted within a day.\n\n## Which file owns what, and one budget\n`docker-storage-cap.sh` owns the BUDGET; `container-cap.sh` owns only the reaper, the two checks\nand the rendering. ADR 0112 puts the writable-layer share INSIDE the Docker budget, so\n`docker-storage-cap.sh` gains a third share `DOCKER_CONTAINER_WRITABLE_BYTES` (2 GiB) carved out\nof the existing 20 GiB, leaving the image share 13 GiB. No new independent literal, and\n`container-cap.sh` reads it rather than re-spelling it. 2 GiB is a starting point under ADR 0112\ndecision 6, labelled as one: no writable-layer figure was captured at the outage (`docker system\ndf`'s Containers row was never recorded), so it is sized from the split, not a measurement.\n\n## Scope\nIn, enumerated: NEW `infra/scripts/container-cap.sh`; `infra/scripts/docker-storage-cap.sh` (the\nthird share); `infra/scripts/observability.sh` (§2i + the extended parse);\n`infra/scripts/compose-up.sh` (installs the timer); `infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf`;\n`infra/runbooks/review-bot-ops.md`; tests. Out: direct `/var/lib/docker/overlay2` deletion, the\nBuildKit cap (S2), journald (S3), `/var/tmp` (S4), the gate-scratch volume.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `--reap` removes an exited unowned container when the footprint exceeds the share, removes\n      nothing under it, reaps oldest-first to an 80% low-water mark, and WARNs naming the running\n      and protected bytes when it cannot get under. (`tests/scripts/test_container_cap.py`)\n- [ ] `--reap` removes nothing for each of: a `running` row; a `com.docker.compose.project`\n      label; a `rebar.service` label; a `rebar-mcp-*` / `compose-mcp-1` name; an exit inside the\n      grace window. Its argv never contains `-f`, `-v`, `--volumes`, `system prune` or\n      `container prune`. A test asserts the protected name pattern equals `mcp_managed`'s regex.\n- [ ] `--reap` removes nothing when the share cannot be read, and says so.\n- [ ] `observability.sh` publishes the three readings from a parseable Containers row and NONE\n      of them when it is absent or unparseable; percent clamped to 100; both heartbeats publish\n      on every tick including their 0 path; all five dimensionless.\n- [ ] `docker-storage-cap.sh --print-env` states the third share, the three shares sum to the\n      budget, and an over-subscribed split is refused.\n- [ ] Both alarms exist by name, `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`, SNS-wired, asserted in\n      `tests/unit/test_alarm_actions_terraform.py`; `terraform fmt -check` and `validate` pass.\n- [ ] The runbook documents diagnosis, remediation, the `rootflags=pquota` + reboot steps, and\n      forbids manual overlay2 deletion.\n- [ ] Portability: Docker runtime APIs, systemd and local pytest only; no CI-provider trigger."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788590137286045001,"uuid":"7670c007-b4de-4ee8-9ed3-55d5b76ec772"}